          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %strong> Extraordinary natural transformations
            , or
            = succeed "," do
              %strong extranatural transformations
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} natural transformation
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformations"} natural transformations
            = succeed "s.)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/antiparticle"} antiparticle
          %p
            A transformation can also be ordinary-natural in some variables and extraordinary-natural in other variables. Sometimes this sort of transformation is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em generalized natural transformation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Kelly"} Max Kelly
            was fond of saying that really it’s all the same basic concept, however, so why proliferate terminology needlessly? So he would simply say a transformation was “natural” in all its arguments, both the “ordinary” and the “extraordinary” ones.
          %p The calculus of natural and extranatural transformations is a very simple string diagram calculus; perhaps the most basic one. It was first introduced by Eilenberg, Kelly, and Mac Lane in the mid 60’s.
          %p
            There is also a yet more general notion of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dinatural+transformation"} dinatural transformation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+categories"} enriched categories
            and do not admit a natural string diagram calculus.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            Consider the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+set"} function set
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom: Set^{op} \times Set \to Set
            (or more generally, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+hom"} internal hom
            functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mi op
                  %mo ×
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom: V^{op} \times V \to V
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+transformation"} identity transformation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mo :
                  %mi hom
                  %mo →
                  %mi hom
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1: \hom \to \hom
            has components of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{x, y}: x^y \to x^y
          %p
            and this of course is natural in each of the separate arguments
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            with a straight line and the two instances of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            with a straight line. Although it’s trivial, let’s at least record what naturality in say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            would mean: it means that for any morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi y
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: y \to y'
            we have an equation of the form
          #eq:naturality.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi g
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ′
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ′
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{x, y} x^g = x^g 1_{x, y'}: x^{y'} \to x^y
          %center
            %img{:src => "http://younesse.net/assets/nLab/extranatural+transformation/svg/naturality.svg", :title => "Xy-Pic code available by replacing /svg/img.svg by /img.md in the image URL", :width => "250"}/
          %p
            Now, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            and internal hom allow us to “bend” the transformation into another:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi eval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi y
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} eval_{x, y}: x^y \otimes y \to x
          %p
            in which the two instances of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are linked by a U-shape. This gives a transformation which is natural in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            but not of course in
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            we have an equation which is companion to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:naturality"} (1)
          #eq:extranaturality.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi eval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi g
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi eval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ′
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi g
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ′
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} eval_{x, y} (x^g \otimes y) = eval_{x, y'} (x^{y'} \otimes g): x^{y'} \otimes y \to x
          %center
            %img{:src => "http://younesse.net/assets/nLab/extranatural+transformation/svg/extranaturality_counit.svg", :title => "Xy-Pic code available by replacing /svg/img.svg by /img.md in the image URL", :width => "350"}/
          %p
            and we say in this case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi eval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} eval_{x, y}
            is
            %strong extranatural
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi eval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} eval_{x, y}
            appears once covariantly [in the tensor factor] and once contravariantly [in the exponent], but together on the same side of the arrow [here the domain]. (There is a nice string diagram picture for
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:extranaturality"} (2)
            which the reader might like to draw at this point.)
          %p
            Thus we already see that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi eval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} eval_{x, y}
            is a “generalized natural” transformation, involving a mixture of naturality (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
          %p The basic idea should now be clear, but let’s give a few more examples. Starting with the identity transformation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo →
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{x, y}: x \otimes y \to x \otimes y
          %p we can again bend it using the tensor-hom adjunction to form an arrow
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi coeval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} coeval_{x, y}: x \to (x \otimes y)^y
          %p
            where again
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            appears once covariantly and once contravariantly, this time on the codomain side. The extranaturality in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            is the condition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi g
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi y
                  %msub
                    %mi coeval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo ′
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi g
                  %msub
                    %mi coeval
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x \otimes g)^{y} coeval_{x, y} = (x \otimes y')^g coeval_{x, y'}
          %center
            %img{:src => "http://younesse.net/assets/nLab/extranatural+transformation/svg/extranaturality_unit.svg", :title => "Xy-Pic code available by replacing /svg/img.svg by /img.md in the image URL", :width => "350"}/
          %p
            for every arrow
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi y
                    %mo →
                    %mi y
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g: y \to y'
          %p As these examples indicate, instances of ordinary naturality are typically transferred into instances of extranaturality by means of adjunctions. Indeed, basic instances of U-shapes or upside-down U-shapes in string diagrams come about through counits and units of adjunctions,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ε
                  %mo :
                  %mi F
                  %mi U
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi η
                  %mo :
                  %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varepsilon: F U \to 1 \qquad \eta: 1 \to U F
          %h2#Defn Formalization
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi B
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi B
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon A\times B\times B^{op} \to D
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi C
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G\colon A\times C\times C^{op}\to D
            be functors. A family of morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi α
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo ,
                      %mi c
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo ,
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_{a,b,c}\colon F(a,b,b) \to G(a,c,c)
          %p
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
            is said to be
            = succeed "," do
              %strong natural
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                ordinary-natural in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
                and extranatural in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
          %ul
            %li
              For all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi a
                    %mo →
                    %mi a
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\colon a\to a'
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              and all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\in B
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
              = succeed "):" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi a
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %mi F
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ′
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi a
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi b
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi c
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi a
                                      %mo ′
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi b
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi c
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi G
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %mi G
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mi f
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mi G
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ′
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{&amp;F(a,b,b) &amp; \overset{F(f,1,1)}{\to} &amp; F(a',b,b) &amp;\\
                      ^{\alpha_{a,b,c}} &amp;\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp; ^{\alpha_{a',b,c}}\\
                      \&amp;G(a,c,c)&amp; \underset{G(f,1,1)}{\to} &amp; G(a',c,c) &amp;}
            %li
              For all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi b
                    %mo →
                    %mi b
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g\colon b\to b'
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
              and all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi c
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c\in C
              = succeed "):" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi b
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mrow
                        %mtable
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mover
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %mi F
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi g
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %mi F
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mi g
                                  %mo ,
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mtd
                              %msup
                                %mo
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi a
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi b
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi c
                          %mtr
                            %mtd
                            %mtd
                              %mi F
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ′
                              %mo ,
                              %mi b
                              %mo ′
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                              %munder
                                %mo →
                                %mrow
                                  %msub
                                    %mi α
                                    %mrow
                                      %mi a
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi b
                                      %mo ′
                                      %mo ,
                                      %mi c
                            %mtd
                              %mi G
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                              %mi a
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mo ,
                              %mi c
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mtd
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\array{&amp;F(a,b,b') &amp; \overset{F(1,1,g)}{\to} &amp; F(a,b,b) &amp;\\
                      ^{F(1,g,1)} &amp;\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow &amp; ^{\alpha_{a,b,c}}\\
                      \&amp;F(a,b',b')&amp; \underset{\alpha_{a,b',c}}{\to} &amp; G(a,c,c) &amp;}
            %li
              For all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi c
                    %mo →
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h\colon c\to c'
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\in A
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
