          %center
            %img{:src => "http://younesse.net/assets/nLab/extranatural+transformation/svg/extranatural+transformation_yanking.svg", :width => "450"}/
          %p In fact, these lemmas essentially capture “all possible” ways in which extranatural transformations can be composed. The general statement, which is obtained by combining these, is that if the graphs representing the two transformations can be composed without creating any cycles, then the transformations can be composed. In some detail, if there are no cycles in the composed graph, then it can be shown that each connected components has one of the forms of the compositions in Lemma 1-3. It then follows from the lemmas (and from the fact that extranaturality can be determined by separation of variables) that each connected component can be replaced by a single edge, and the resulting graph defines the type of the composed transformation.
          %p This can be found in the original paper about extranatural transformations:
          %ul
            %li
              Eilenberg and Kelly,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A generalization of the functorial calculus
          %p
            With the operation of “loop-free composition,” extranatural transformations with a given target form a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/paracategory"} paracategory
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                extraordinary 2-multicategory
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/extraordinary+2-multicategory"} ?
          %h2#profunctors Profunctors
          %p
            One abstract way to describe the structure of extranatural transformations is as an
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                extraordinary 2-multicategory
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/extraordinary+2-multicategory"} ?
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed
            monoidal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proarrow+equipment"} proarrow equipment
          %p
            More should go here, but for now see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+double+category"} compact closed double category
